  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses only on ordinal comparisons of maximum and minimum outcomes (i.e., which lottery has a higher max, higher min), not on the magnitude of differences or expected value calculations. The individual, however, considers not just that B has a higher outcome, but also compares the size of the difference in outcomes versus probabilities and invokes a 'statistically more money' argument, which suggests an expectation-based or at least a weighted trade-off reasoning. This goes beyond the simple count of favorable comparisons the decision reason uses. While both the reason and the individual note that B has a higher maximum outcome, the individual does not mention or act on a comparison of minimum outcomes (which are equal), nor do they frame their decision in terms of 'more favorable comparisons'. Instead, their justification reflects a sensitivity to both outcome and probability magnitudes, inconsistent with the lexicographic or count-based nature of the decision reason. Therefore, although there is partial alignment in noting the higher maximum in B, the reasoning process differs in kind.",
